{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824940255,"sessionID":"ses_f461f2da0ffepmGxMLqn7fNdlL","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9e13835001i187aF42SWFwil","sessionID":"ses_f461f2da0ffepmGxMLqn7fNdlL","messageID":"msg_0b9e1317e001fq5sC89B6XBLh4","type":"tool","id":"call_00_dXiQBcdP5zl8yOnoJMBX9959","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":250,"offset":580,"path":"/tmp/opencode/pw/filing.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pw/filing.txt, lines 580-829\n580: “preferred stock” refers to preferred stock, par value $50.00 per share, of the Company; the term “merger” refers to the merger of Merger Sub with and into the Company with the Company surviving as a wholly-owned subsidiary of\n581: G&W; the term “surviving corporation” refers to the Company, following completion of the merger; and the term “merger agreement” refers to the Merger Agreement, dated as of August 12, 2016, by and among G&W, the Company\n582: and Merger Sub, a copy of which is included as Annex A to this proxy statement and incorporated into this proxy statement by reference, as amended or modified from time to time. \n583: \n584:  The Parties to the Merger \n585: \n586:  Providence and Worcester Railroad Company (page 21) \n587: \n588:  The Company, a Rhode Island corporation, is a regional freight railroad operating in Massachusetts, Rhode Island, Connecticut and New York. The\n589: Company is the only interstate freight carrier serving the State of Rhode Island and possesses the exclusive and perpetual right to conduct freight operations over the National Railroad Passenger Corporation’s (Amtrak) Northeast Corridor\n590: between New Haven, Connecticut and the Massachusetts/Rhode Island border. Since commencing independent operations in 1973, the Company, through a series of acquisitions of connecting lines and trackage rights agreements, has grown from 45 miles of\n591: track to its current system of approximately 516 miles. The Company services the largest international double-stack intermodal terminal facility in New England in Worcester, Massachusetts, a strategic location for regional transportation and\n592: distribution enterprises. The Company’s common stock is listed on the Nasdaq Global Select Market (“NASDAQ”) under the ticker symbol “PWX.” The Company’s principal executive offices are located at 75 Hammond\n593: Street, Worcester, Massachusetts 01610, its telephone number is (508) 755-4000, and its website is www.pwrr.com. \n594:  Additional\n595: information about the Company is included in documents incorporated by reference into this proxy statement and our filings with the Securities and Exchange Commission (the “SEC”). \n596: \n597:  Genesee & Wyoming Inc. (page 21) \n598: \n599:  G&W, a Delaware corporation, owns or leases 121 freight railroads worldwide that are organized in 10 operating regions with approximately\n600: 7,200 employees and more than 2,800 customers. G&W’s eight North American regions serve 41 U.S. states and four Canadian provinces and include 114 short line and regional freight railroads with more than 13,000 track-miles. G&W’s\n601: Australia Region provides rail freight services in New South Wales, the Northern Territory and South Australia and operates the 1,400-mile Tarcoola-to-Darwin rail line. G&W’s U.K./Europe Region is led by Freightliner, the U.K.’s\n602: largest rail maritime intermodal operator and second-largest rail freight company and whose operations also include heavy-haul in Poland and Germany and cross-border intermodal services connecting Northern European seaports with key industrial\n603: regions \n604: \n605:  1 \n606: \n607:  Table of Contents \n608: \n609: throughout the continent. G&W subsidiaries provide rail service at more than 40 major ports in North America, Australia and Europe and perform contract coal loading and railcar switching for\n610: industrial customers. \n611:  G&W’s common stock is listed on the New York Stock Exchange under the ticker symbol “GWR”.\n612: G&W’s principal executive offices are located at 20 West Avenue, Darien, Connecticut 06820, its telephone number is (203) 202-8900, and its website is www.gwrr.com. \n613: \n614:  Pullman Acquisition Sub Inc. (page 21) \n615: \n616:  Merger Sub, a Rhode Island corporation, is a wholly-owned subsidiary of G&W, and was formed by G&W on August 12, 2016 solely for the\n617: purposes of entering into the merger agreement and effecting the merger. Merger Sub has not conducted any business operations other than those incidental to its formation and in connection with the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement.\n618: Upon completion of the merger, Merger Sub will merge with and into the Company and Merger Sub will cease to exist as a separate entity. Merger Sub’s principal executive offices are located at 20 West Avenue, Darien, Connecticut 06820, and its\n619: telephone number is (203) 202-8900. \n620:  The Special Meeting of the Shareholders \n621: \n622:  Date, Time and Place of the Special Meeting of Shareholders (page 22) \n623: \n624:  The special meeting of shareholders of the Company will be held at Union Station, Two Washington Square, Worcester, Massachusetts 01604, on\n625: Wednesday, October 26, 2016 at 10:00 a.m., local time. \n626:  Actions to be Taken at the Special Meeting (page 22) \n627: \n628:  At the special meeting, the Company’s shareholders as of September 16, 2016 (the “record date”) will be asked to vote upon a\n629: proposal to approve the merger agreement, a proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, the compensation that may be paid or become payable by the Company to the named executive officers of the Company in connection with, or following\n630: the completion of, the merger (the “non-binding compensation proposal”) and a proposal to approve an adjournment of the special meeting of the Company’s shareholders (the “special meeting”), if necessary, to permit further\n631: solicitation of proxies if there are not sufficient votes at the time of the special meeting to approve the merger agreement (the “adjournment proposal”). \n632: \n633:  The Company’s shareholders may also be asked to transact such other business as may properly come before the special meeting or any\n634: adjournment or postponement of the special meeting. \n635:  The Company’s shareholders must vote to approve the merger proposal as a\n636: condition for the merger to occur. If the Company’s shareholders fail to approve the merger proposal by the requisite vote, the merger will not occur. \n637: \n638:  Record Date; Outstanding Shares; Shares Entitled to Vote (page 22) \n639: \n640:  Only holders of record of common stock and preferred stock at the close of business on the record date of September 16, 2016 are entitled to\n641: notice of and to vote at the special meeting. As of the record date, there were 4,866,593 shares of common stock outstanding, held of record by approximately 540 shareholders, and there were 640 shares of preferred stock outstanding, held of record\n642: by 8 shareholders. Each holder of common stock is entitled to one vote for each share of common stock that he, she or it owned as of the record date. Each holder of preferred stock is entitled to one vote for each share of preferred stock that he,\n643: she or it owned as of the record date. \n644: \n645:  2 \n646: \n647:  Table of Contents \n648: \n649:  Recommendation of the Board of Directors (page 22) \n650: \n651:  At a meeting on August 12, 2016, after consulting with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor and after consideration of various\n652: factors more fully described in this proxy statement, the Company’s board of directors (the “Board”) (i) determined that the merger agreement, the plan of merger and the merger and other transactions contemplated by the merger\n653: agreement are fair to, advisable and in the best interests of the Company and its shareholders, (ii) approved and declared advisable the execution, delivery and performance of the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated thereby, including\n654: the merger and the plan of merger, (iii) directed that the merger agreement be submitted to the shareholders for approval and (iv) resolved to recommend that the shareholders approve the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated thereby,\n655: including the merger and the plan of merger . The Board recommends that you vote “FOR” the merger proposal, “ FOR ” the non-binding compensation proposal, and “ FOR ” the adjournment proposal. \n656: \n657:  Votes Required to Transact Business at the Special Meeting (page 22) \n658: \n659:  A quorum of the Company’s shareholders is necessary to hold a valid meeting. If the holders of at least a majority of the total number of\n660: the outstanding shares of each class of Company stock entitled to vote are represented in person or by proxy at the special meeting, a quorum will exist. The Company will include proxies marked as abstentions in determining the presence of a quorum\n661: at the special meeting. \n662:  The affirmative vote of (1) the holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of common stock, and (2)\n663: the holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of preferred stock, voting as separate classes and not together, is required to approve the merger agreement. Additionally, the affirmative vote of (1) the holders of at least a majority\n664: of the outstanding shares of common stock present in person or represented by proxy at the special meeting and (2) the holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of preferred stock present in person or represented by proxy at the\n665: special meeting, voting as separate classes and not together, is required to approve the non-binding compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal. \n666: \n667:  How to Vote Shares Held Directly by the Shareholder (page 23) \n668: \n669:  The Board requests that you vote your shares by returning the proxy card accompanying this document for use at the special meeting. If you\n670: choose to vote by mail, please complete, date and sign the proxy card and promptly return it in the enclosed pre-paid envelope. All properly signed proxies received prior to the special meeting and not revoked before the vote at the special meeting\n671: will be voted at the special meeting according to the instructions indicated on the proxies or, if no instructions are given, to approve the merger agreement, the non-binding compensation proposal, and the adjournment proposal. If you fail to submit\n672: a proxy or to vote in person at the special meeting, or do not provide your broker, bank or other nominee with instructions, as applicable, your shares of preferred stock or common stock, as applicable, will not be voted on the proposals, which will\n673: have the same effect as a vote “ AGAINST ” the proposal to approve the merger agreement, but will have no effect on the other proposals. \n674: \n675:  How to Vote Shares Held by a Broker, Bank or Other Nominee (page 24) \n676: \n677:  If your shares are held through a broker, bank or other nominee, you may vote your shares by completing, signing and dating the voting\n678: instruction form provided to you by your broker, bank or other nominee. You may also be able to vote your shares via telephone or the internet in accordance with the instructions provided by your broker, bank or nominee. To be able to vote shares\n679: not registered in your own name in person at the special meeting, you will need appropriate documentation from the record holder of your shares. If you hold your shares in “street name” through a broker or bank, you may only vote or change\n680: your vote in person if you have a legal proxy in your name from your broker or bank. \n681: \n682:  3 \n683: \n684:  Table of Contents \n685: \n686:  Revocation of Proxies (page 25) \n687: \n688:  If you have not voted through your broker, bank or other nominee, you may revoke your proxy at any time by taking any of the following actions\n689: before your proxy is voted at the special meeting: \n690: \n691:  • \n692: \n693:  Timely filing a written revocation of the proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the Company, Charles D. Rennick, Providence and Worcester Railroad Company, 75 Hammond Street, Worcester, Massachusetts 01610;\n694: \n695:  • \n696: \n697:  Timely submitting a new signed proxy card bearing a later date (any earlier proxies will be revoked automatically); or \n698: \n699:  • \n700: \n701:  Attending and voting in person at the special meeting provided you are the holder of record of your shares and have filed a written revocation of your grant of proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the Company\n702: as indicated above. \n703: \n704:  If you have instructed a bank, broker or other nominee to vote your shares, you must follow the\n705: directions you receive from your bank, broker or other nominee to change your vote. \n706:  Stock Certificates ( page 25) \n707: \n708:  As soon as practicable after the effective time of the merger, a paying agent will mail a letter of transmittal and instructions to you\n709: and the other shareholders. The letter of transmittal and instructions will tell you how to surrender your stock certificates in exchange for the merger consideration. Holders of uncertificated shares of common stock ( i.e. , holders whose\n710: shares are held in book-entry form) will receive the merger consideration, without interest and subject to reduction for any required withholding taxes, as promptly as practicable after the effective time of the merger without any further action\n711: required on the part of those holders, unless the paying agent requires that such holders first deliver an executed letter of transmittal to the paying agent in order to receive the merger consideration that such holder is entitled to receive. \n712: \n713:  Share Ownership of Management (page 26) \n714: \n715:  As of the record date, the directors and executive officers of the Company and their affiliates collectively beneficially owned 1,107,002\n716: shares of common stock (excluding common stock issuable upon conversion of the preferred stock) and 500 shares of preferred stock, or approximately 22.7% of the outstanding common stock and approximately 78.1% of the outstanding preferred stock.\n717: \n718:  Voting Agreement (page 26) \n719: \n720:  The Robert H. Eder Trust and the Linda Eder Trust, which we refer to as the “Eder Trusts,” have executed a voting agreement with\n721: G&W and the Company under which the Eder Trusts have agreed to vote their shares in favor of approving the merger agreement. As of the record date, the Eder Trusts held 842,742 shares of common stock, representing approximately 17.3% of the\n722: outstanding shares of common stock, and 500 shares of preferred stock, representing approximately 78.1% of the outstanding shares of preferred stock. \n723: \n724:  Delisting and Deregistration of Our Common Stock (page 43) \n725: \n726:  If the merger is completed, our common stock will be delisted from the NASDAQ and deregistered under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as\n727: amended (the “Exchange Act”) and we will no longer file periodic reports with the SEC on account of our common stock. \n728: \n729:  4 \n730: \n731:  Table of Contents \n732: \n733:  The Merger \n734: \n735:  Structure of the Merger (page 50) \n736: \n737:  G&W, Merger Sub and the Company entered into the merger agreement on August 12, 2016. The merger agreement provides for the merger of\n738: Merger Sub with and into the Company, with the Company being the surviving corporation and becoming a wholly-owned subsidiary of G&W. \n739: \n740:  The proposed merger will occur following approval of the merger proposal described in this document by the shareholders of the Company (with\n741: holders of common stock and preferred stock each voting as a separate class) and satisfaction or waiver of all other conditions to the merger. The merger agreement is attached to this proxy statement as Annex A . We encourage you to read the\n742: merger agreement because it is the legal document that governs the merger. \n743:  Closing of the Merger (page 50) \n744: \n745:  We expect that the merger will be completed as soon as practicable following the satisfaction or waiver of all closing conditions, including\n746: approval of the merger agreement by each class of the Company’s shareholders at the special meeting and receipt of all regulatory approvals. The parties cannot be certain whether or when any of the conditions to the merger will be satisfied, or\n747: waived where permissible. We currently expect to complete the merger during the fourth calendar quarter of 2016; however, because the merger is subject to these conditions, we cannot predict the actual timing, or whether the closing of the merger\n748: will occur at all. \n749:  Merger Consideration (page 50) \n750: \n751:  If the merger is completed, at the effective time of the merger, each issued and outstanding share of preferred stock immediately prior to the\n752: effective time of the merger (other than shares of preferred stock held by shareholders who are entitled to demand and make a proper demand for payment of the fair value of such preferred stock pursuant to such holders’ dissenters’ rights\n753: under Rhode Island law) will be deemed to be automatically converted, along with the aggregate accrued or accumulated and unpaid dividends thereon, into 100 shares of common stock (based on the existing conversion ratio of the preferred stock),\n754: without any further action by the holder of such preferred stock or the Company, and each share of common stock (including common stock into which any preferred stock has been deemed converted, but excluding shares that are owned by\n755: G&W or any of its direct or indirect subsidiaries, including Merger Sub) will be canceled and will be converted automatically into the right to receive $25.00 in cash, without interest, less any applicable withholding taxes. \n756: \n757:  Treatment of Equity and Equity-Based Awards (page 51) \n758: \n759:  At the effective time of the merger, subject to all required withholding taxes: \n760: \n761:  • \n762: \n763:  each option to purchase common stock that is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, whether vested or unvested, will be canceled and converted into the right to receive a cash payment equal\n764: to the product of (x) the number of shares of common stock underlying such unexercised stock option; and (y) the excess, if any, of $25.00 over the exercise price per share provided in such option, without interest; and \n765: \n766:  • \n767: \n768:  each restricted stock unit, whether vested or unvested, that is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger will be canceled and will be converted automatically into the right to receive a cash\n769: payment equal to the product of (x) the number of shares of common stock subject to such restricted stock unit and (y) $25.00, without interest. \n770: \n771:  5 \n772: \n773:  Table of Contents \n774: \n775:  Opinion of the Company’s Financial Advisor (page 36) \n776: \n777:  In connection with the merger, at the meeting of the Board on August 12, 2016, our financial advisor, BMO Capital Markets Corp., which we refer\n778: to as “BMO,” rendered to the Board its oral opinion, subsequently confirmed by delivery of a written opinion dated August 12, 2016, as to the fairness, from a financial point of view, to the holders of our common stock (other than the Eder\n779: Trusts) as of that date and based upon and subject to the various assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and qualifications and limitations on the scope of review undertaken by BMO as set forth in its written opinion, of the\n780: merger consideration per share of our common stock to be received by those holders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. \n781: \n782:  The full text of BMO’s written opinion, dated August 12, 2016, is attached to this proxy statement as\n783:  Annex C and is incorporated into this proxy statement by reference. You should read BMO’s opinion carefully and in its entirety for a discussion of the assumptions made, procedures followed,\n784: matters considered and qualifications and limitations on the scope of the review undertaken by BMO in rendering its opinion. This summary is qualified in its entirety by reference to the full text of the opinion. BMO’s opinion was\n785: directed to the Board, in its capacity as the Board, and addressed only the fairness from a financial point of view, as of the date of the opinion, to the holders of our common stock (which, for the avoidance of doubt, included the preferred stock\n786: on an as-converted basis), other than the Eder Trusts, of the consideration to be received by those shareholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. The opinion did not address any other aspects or implications of the merger and did not\n787: address the relative merits of the merger contemplated by the merger agreement as compared to other business or financial strategies that might have been available, nor did it address the underlying business decision to enter into the merger\n788: agreement or proceed with any other transaction contemplated by the merger agreement. BMO’s opinion was not intended to, and does not, constitute advice or a recommendation as to how any holder of our common stock should vote at the special\n789: meeting or take any other action with respect to the merger. For more information regarding our financial advisor, see the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal No. 1—The Merger—Opinion of the Company’s Financial\n790: Advisor” beginning on page 36. \n791:  Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger (page 44) \n792: \n793:  In considering the recommendation of the Board that you vote to approve the merger agreement, you should be aware that Company directors\n794: and executive officers may be deemed to have interests in the merger that are different from, or in addition to, the interests of the Company’s shareholders generally and which may create potential conflicts of interest. The members of the\n795: Board were aware of and considered these interests in reaching the determination to approve the merger agreement and deem the merger agreement, the merger and the other transactions contemplated by the merger agreement to be advisable and in the\n796: best interests of the Company, and in recommending that the Company’s shareholders vote for the approval of the merger agreement. These interests include \n797: \n798:  • \n799: \n800:  each Company stock option and restricted stock unit, whether vested or unvested, will be canceled and converted into the right to receive cash payments based on the merger consideration upon the effective time of the\n801: merger in accordance with the terms of the merger agreement; \n802: \n803:  • \n804: \n805:  each of the Company’s executive officers (other than Robert H. Eder, our Chief Executive Officer) is party to a change in control agreement with the Company that provides severance in the case of a termination\n806: of employment by the Company without “cause” or by the executive for “good reason” in connection with or following a change in control, which will include the completion of the merger; and \n807: \n808:  • \n809: \n810:  the Company’s directors and executive officers are entitled to continued indemnification and insurance coverage under the merger agreement. \n811: \n812:  6 \n813: \n814:  Table of Contents \n815: \n816:  The merger agreement also provides that the Company’s executive officers immediately prior to the\n817: effective time of the merger (excluding our Chief Executive Officer) will remain in office following the merger until their successors are duly elected or appointed and qualified. Please see the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal\n818: No. 1—The Merger—Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger” beginning on page 44 for additional information about these financial interests. \n819: \n820:  Conditions to the Merger (page 52) \n821: \n822:  The obligations of G&W and the Company to effect the merger are subject to the satisfaction or waiver, at or prior to the closing of the\n823: merger, of a number of conditions, including: \n824: \n825:  • \n826: \n827:  holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of common stock and preferred stock, each voting separately as a class, have approved the merger agreement and the plan of merger; \n828: \n829:  • \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 830]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pw/filing.txt, lines 580-829\n580: “preferred stock” refers to preferred stock, par value $50.00 per share, of the Company; the term “merger” refers to the merger of Merger Sub with and into the Company with the Company surviving as a wholly-owned subsidiary of\n581: G&W; the term “surviving corporation” refers to the Company, following completion of the merger; and the term “merger agreement” refers to the Merger Agreement, dated as of August 12, 2016, by and among G&W, the Company\n582: and Merger Sub, a copy of which is included as Annex A to this proxy statement and incorporated into this proxy statement by reference, as amended or modified from time to time. \n583: \n584:  The Parties to the Merger \n585: \n586:  Providence and Worcester Railroad Company (page 21) \n587: \n588:  The Company, a Rhode Island corporation, is a regional freight railroad operating in Massachusetts, Rhode Island, Connecticut and New York. The\n589: Company is the only interstate freight carrier serving the State of Rhode Island and possesses the exclusive and perpetual right to conduct freight operations over the National Railroad Passenger Corporation’s (Amtrak) Northeast Corridor\n590: between New Haven, Connecticut and the Massachusetts/Rhode Island border. Since commencing independent operations in 1973, the Company, through a series of acquisitions of connecting lines and trackage rights agreements, has grown from 45 miles of\n591: track to its current system of approximately 516 miles. The Company services the largest international double-stack intermodal terminal facility in New England in Worcester, Massachusetts, a strategic location for regional transportation and\n592: distribution enterprises. The Company’s common stock is listed on the Nasdaq Global Select Market (“NASDAQ”) under the ticker symbol “PWX.” The Company’s principal executive offices are located at 75 Hammond\n593: Street, Worcester, Massachusetts 01610, its telephone number is (508) 755-4000, and its website is www.pwrr.com. \n594:  Additional\n595: information about the Company is included in documents incorporated by reference into this proxy statement and our filings with the Securities and Exchange Commission (the “SEC”). \n596: \n597:  Genesee & Wyoming Inc. (page 21) \n598: \n599:  G&W, a Delaware corporation, owns or leases 121 freight railroads worldwide that are organized in 10 operating regions with approximately\n600: 7,200 employees and more than 2,800 customers. G&W’s eight North American regions serve 41 U.S. states and four Canadian provinces and include 114 short line and regional freight railroads with more than 13,000 track-miles. G&W’s\n601: Australia Region provides rail freight services in New South Wales, the Northern Territory and South Australia and operates the 1,400-mile Tarcoola-to-Darwin rail line. G&W’s U.K./Europe Region is led by Freightliner, the U.K.’s\n602: largest rail maritime intermodal operator and second-largest rail freight company and whose operations also include heavy-haul in Poland and Germany and cross-border intermodal services connecting Northern European seaports with key industrial\n603: regions \n604: \n605:  1 \n606: \n607:  Table of Contents \n608: \n609: throughout the continent. G&W subsidiaries provide rail service at more than 40 major ports in North America, Australia and Europe and perform contract coal loading and railcar switching for\n610: industrial customers. \n611:  G&W’s common stock is listed on the New York Stock Exchange under the ticker symbol “GWR”.\n612: G&W’s principal executive offices are located at 20 West Avenue, Darien, Connecticut 06820, its telephone number is (203) 202-8900, and its website is www.gwrr.com. \n613: \n614:  Pullman Acquisition Sub Inc. (page 21) \n615: \n616:  Merger Sub, a Rhode Island corporation, is a wholly-owned subsidiary of G&W, and was formed by G&W on August 12, 2016 solely for the\n617: purposes of entering into the merger agreement and effecting the merger. Merger Sub has not conducted any business operations other than those incidental to its formation and in connection with the transactions contemplated by the merger agreement.\n618: Upon completion of the merger, Merger Sub will merge with and into the Company and Merger Sub will cease to exist as a separate entity. Merger Sub’s principal executive offices are located at 20 West Avenue, Darien, Connecticut 06820, and its\n619: telephone number is (203) 202-8900. \n620:  The Special Meeting of the Shareholders \n621: \n622:  Date, Time and Place of the Special Meeting of Shareholders (page 22) \n623: \n624:  The special meeting of shareholders of the Company will be held at Union Station, Two Washington Square, Worcester, Massachusetts 01604, on\n625: Wednesday, October 26, 2016 at 10:00 a.m., local time. \n626:  Actions to be Taken at the Special Meeting (page 22) \n627: \n628:  At the special meeting, the Company’s shareholders as of September 16, 2016 (the “record date”) will be asked to vote upon a\n629: proposal to approve the merger agreement, a proposal to approve, on an advisory (non-binding) basis, the compensation that may be paid or become payable by the Company to the named executive officers of the Company in connection with, or following\n630: the completion of, the merger (the “non-binding compensation proposal”) and a proposal to approve an adjournment of the special meeting of the Company’s shareholders (the “special meeting”), if necessary, to permit further\n631: solicitation of proxies if there are not sufficient votes at the time of the special meeting to approve the merger agreement (the “adjournment proposal”). \n632: \n633:  The Company’s shareholders may also be asked to transact such other business as may properly come before the special meeting or any\n634: adjournment or postponement of the special meeting. \n635:  The Company’s shareholders must vote to approve the merger proposal as a\n636: condition for the merger to occur. If the Company’s shareholders fail to approve the merger proposal by the requisite vote, the merger will not occur. \n637: \n638:  Record Date; Outstanding Shares; Shares Entitled to Vote (page 22) \n639: \n640:  Only holders of record of common stock and preferred stock at the close of business on the record date of September 16, 2016 are entitled to\n641: notice of and to vote at the special meeting. As of the record date, there were 4,866,593 shares of common stock outstanding, held of record by approximately 540 shareholders, and there were 640 shares of preferred stock outstanding, held of record\n642: by 8 shareholders. Each holder of common stock is entitled to one vote for each share of common stock that he, she or it owned as of the record date. Each holder of preferred stock is entitled to one vote for each share of preferred stock that he,\n643: she or it owned as of the record date. \n644: \n645:  2 \n646: \n647:  Table of Contents \n648: \n649:  Recommendation of the Board of Directors (page 22) \n650: \n651:  At a meeting on August 12, 2016, after consulting with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor and after consideration of various\n652: factors more fully described in this proxy statement, the Company’s board of directors (the “Board”) (i) determined that the merger agreement, the plan of merger and the merger and other transactions contemplated by the merger\n653: agreement are fair to, advisable and in the best interests of the Company and its shareholders, (ii) approved and declared advisable the execution, delivery and performance of the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated thereby, including\n654: the merger and the plan of merger, (iii) directed that the merger agreement be submitted to the shareholders for approval and (iv) resolved to recommend that the shareholders approve the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated thereby,\n655: including the merger and the plan of merger . The Board recommends that you vote “FOR” the merger proposal, “ FOR ” the non-binding compensation proposal, and “ FOR ” the adjournment proposal. \n656: \n657:  Votes Required to Transact Business at the Special Meeting (page 22) \n658: \n659:  A quorum of the Company’s shareholders is necessary to hold a valid meeting. If the holders of at least a majority of the total number of\n660: the outstanding shares of each class of Company stock entitled to vote are represented in person or by proxy at the special meeting, a quorum will exist. The Company will include proxies marked as abstentions in determining the presence of a quorum\n661: at the special meeting. \n662:  The affirmative vote of (1) the holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of common stock, and (2)\n663: the holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of preferred stock, voting as separate classes and not together, is required to approve the merger agreement. Additionally, the affirmative vote of (1) the holders of at least a majority\n664: of the outstanding shares of common stock present in person or represented by proxy at the special meeting and (2) the holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of preferred stock present in person or represented by proxy at the\n665: special meeting, voting as separate classes and not together, is required to approve the non-binding compensation proposal and the adjournment proposal. \n666: \n667:  How to Vote Shares Held Directly by the Shareholder (page 23) \n668: \n669:  The Board requests that you vote your shares by returning the proxy card accompanying this document for use at the special meeting. If you\n670: choose to vote by mail, please complete, date and sign the proxy card and promptly return it in the enclosed pre-paid envelope. All properly signed proxies received prior to the special meeting and not revoked before the vote at the special meeting\n671: will be voted at the special meeting according to the instructions indicated on the proxies or, if no instructions are given, to approve the merger agreement, the non-binding compensation proposal, and the adjournment proposal. If you fail to submit\n672: a proxy or to vote in person at the special meeting, or do not provide your broker, bank or other nominee with instructions, as applicable, your shares of preferred stock or common stock, as applicable, will not be voted on the proposals, which will\n673: have the same effect as a vote “ AGAINST ” the proposal to approve the merger agreement, but will have no effect on the other proposals. \n674: \n675:  How to Vote Shares Held by a Broker, Bank or Other Nominee (page 24) \n676: \n677:  If your shares are held through a broker, bank or other nominee, you may vote your shares by completing, signing and dating the voting\n678: instruction form provided to you by your broker, bank or other nominee. You may also be able to vote your shares via telephone or the internet in accordance with the instructions provided by your broker, bank or nominee. To be able to vote shares\n679: not registered in your own name in person at the special meeting, you will need appropriate documentation from the record holder of your shares. If you hold your shares in “street name” through a broker or bank, you may only vote or change\n680: your vote in person if you have a legal proxy in your name from your broker or bank. \n681: \n682:  3 \n683: \n684:  Table of Contents \n685: \n686:  Revocation of Proxies (page 25) \n687: \n688:  If you have not voted through your broker, bank or other nominee, you may revoke your proxy at any time by taking any of the following actions\n689: before your proxy is voted at the special meeting: \n690: \n691:  • \n692: \n693:  Timely filing a written revocation of the proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the Company, Charles D. Rennick, Providence and Worcester Railroad Company, 75 Hammond Street, Worcester, Massachusetts 01610;\n694: \n695:  • \n696: \n697:  Timely submitting a new signed proxy card bearing a later date (any earlier proxies will be revoked automatically); or \n698: \n699:  • \n700: \n701:  Attending and voting in person at the special meeting provided you are the holder of record of your shares and have filed a written revocation of your grant of proxy with the Secretary and General Counsel of the Company\n702: as indicated above. \n703: \n704:  If you have instructed a bank, broker or other nominee to vote your shares, you must follow the\n705: directions you receive from your bank, broker or other nominee to change your vote. \n706:  Stock Certificates ( page 25) \n707: \n708:  As soon as practicable after the effective time of the merger, a paying agent will mail a letter of transmittal and instructions to you\n709: and the other shareholders. The letter of transmittal and instructions will tell you how to surrender your stock certificates in exchange for the merger consideration. Holders of uncertificated shares of common stock ( i.e. , holders whose\n710: shares are held in book-entry form) will receive the merger consideration, without interest and subject to reduction for any required withholding taxes, as promptly as practicable after the effective time of the merger without any further action\n711: required on the part of those holders, unless the paying agent requires that such holders first deliver an executed letter of transmittal to the paying agent in order to receive the merger consideration that such holder is entitled to receive. \n712: \n713:  Share Ownership of Management (page 26) \n714: \n715:  As of the record date, the directors and executive officers of the Company and their affiliates collectively beneficially owned 1,107,002\n716: shares of common stock (excluding common stock issuable upon conversion of the preferred stock) and 500 shares of preferred stock, or approximately 22.7% of the outstanding common stock and approximately 78.1% of the outstanding preferred stock.\n717: \n718:  Voting Agreement (page 26) \n719: \n720:  The Robert H. Eder Trust and the Linda Eder Trust, which we refer to as the “Eder Trusts,” have executed a voting agreement with\n721: G&W and the Company under which the Eder Trusts have agreed to vote their shares in favor of approving the merger agreement. As of the record date, the Eder Trusts held 842,742 shares of common stock, representing approximately 17.3% of the\n722: outstanding shares of common stock, and 500 shares of preferred stock, representing approximately 78.1% of the outstanding shares of preferred stock. \n723: \n724:  Delisting and Deregistration of Our Common Stock (page 43) \n725: \n726:  If the merger is completed, our common stock will be delisted from the NASDAQ and deregistered under the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as\n727: amended (the “Exchange Act”) and we will no longer file periodic reports with the SEC on account of our common stock. \n728: \n729:  4 \n730: \n731:  Table of Contents \n732: \n733:  The Merger \n734: \n735:  Structure of the Merger (page 50) \n736: \n737:  G&W, Merger Sub and the Company entered into the merger agreement on August 12, 2016. The merger agreement provides for the merger of\n738: Merger Sub with and into the Company, with the Company being the surviving corporation and becoming a wholly-owned subsidiary of G&W. \n739: \n740:  The proposed merger will occur following approval of the merger proposal described in this document by the shareholders of the Company (with\n741: holders of common stock and preferred stock each voting as a separate class) and satisfaction or waiver of all other conditions to the merger. The merger agreement is attached to this proxy statement as Annex A . We encourage you to read the\n742: merger agreement because it is the legal document that governs the merger. \n743:  Closing of the Merger (page 50) \n744: \n745:  We expect that the merger will be completed as soon as practicable following the satisfaction or waiver of all closing conditions, including\n746: approval of the merger agreement by each class of the Company’s shareholders at the special meeting and receipt of all regulatory approvals. The parties cannot be certain whether or when any of the conditions to the merger will be satisfied, or\n747: waived where permissible. We currently expect to complete the merger during the fourth calendar quarter of 2016; however, because the merger is subject to these conditions, we cannot predict the actual timing, or whether the closing of the merger\n748: will occur at all. \n749:  Merger Consideration (page 50) \n750: \n751:  If the merger is completed, at the effective time of the merger, each issued and outstanding share of preferred stock immediately prior to the\n752: effective time of the merger (other than shares of preferred stock held by shareholders who are entitled to demand and make a proper demand for payment of the fair value of such preferred stock pursuant to such holders’ dissenters’ rights\n753: under Rhode Island law) will be deemed to be automatically converted, along with the aggregate accrued or accumulated and unpaid dividends thereon, into 100 shares of common stock (based on the existing conversion ratio of the preferred stock),\n754: without any further action by the holder of such preferred stock or the Company, and each share of common stock (including common stock into which any preferred stock has been deemed converted, but excluding shares that are owned by\n755: G&W or any of its direct or indirect subsidiaries, including Merger Sub) will be canceled and will be converted automatically into the right to receive $25.00 in cash, without interest, less any applicable withholding taxes. \n756: \n757:  Treatment of Equity and Equity-Based Awards (page 51) \n758: \n759:  At the effective time of the merger, subject to all required withholding taxes: \n760: \n761:  • \n762: \n763:  each option to purchase common stock that is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger, whether vested or unvested, will be canceled and converted into the right to receive a cash payment equal\n764: to the product of (x) the number of shares of common stock underlying such unexercised stock option; and (y) the excess, if any, of $25.00 over the exercise price per share provided in such option, without interest; and \n765: \n766:  • \n767: \n768:  each restricted stock unit, whether vested or unvested, that is outstanding immediately prior to the effective time of the merger will be canceled and will be converted automatically into the right to receive a cash\n769: payment equal to the product of (x) the number of shares of common stock subject to such restricted stock unit and (y) $25.00, without interest. \n770: \n771:  5 \n772: \n773:  Table of Contents \n774: \n775:  Opinion of the Company’s Financial Advisor (page 36) \n776: \n777:  In connection with the merger, at the meeting of the Board on August 12, 2016, our financial advisor, BMO Capital Markets Corp., which we refer\n778: to as “BMO,” rendered to the Board its oral opinion, subsequently confirmed by delivery of a written opinion dated August 12, 2016, as to the fairness, from a financial point of view, to the holders of our common stock (other than the Eder\n779: Trusts) as of that date and based upon and subject to the various assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and qualifications and limitations on the scope of review undertaken by BMO as set forth in its written opinion, of the\n780: merger consideration per share of our common stock to be received by those holders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. \n781: \n782:  The full text of BMO’s written opinion, dated August 12, 2016, is attached to this proxy statement as\n783:  Annex C and is incorporated into this proxy statement by reference. You should read BMO’s opinion carefully and in its entirety for a discussion of the assumptions made, procedures followed,\n784: matters considered and qualifications and limitations on the scope of the review undertaken by BMO in rendering its opinion. This summary is qualified in its entirety by reference to the full text of the opinion. BMO’s opinion was\n785: directed to the Board, in its capacity as the Board, and addressed only the fairness from a financial point of view, as of the date of the opinion, to the holders of our common stock (which, for the avoidance of doubt, included the preferred stock\n786: on an as-converted basis), other than the Eder Trusts, of the consideration to be received by those shareholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. The opinion did not address any other aspects or implications of the merger and did not\n787: address the relative merits of the merger contemplated by the merger agreement as compared to other business or financial strategies that might have been available, nor did it address the underlying business decision to enter into the merger\n788: agreement or proceed with any other transaction contemplated by the merger agreement. BMO’s opinion was not intended to, and does not, constitute advice or a recommendation as to how any holder of our common stock should vote at the special\n789: meeting or take any other action with respect to the merger. For more information regarding our financial advisor, see the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal No. 1—The Merger—Opinion of the Company’s Financial\n790: Advisor” beginning on page 36. \n791:  Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger (page 44) \n792: \n793:  In considering the recommendation of the Board that you vote to approve the merger agreement, you should be aware that Company directors\n794: and executive officers may be deemed to have interests in the merger that are different from, or in addition to, the interests of the Company’s shareholders generally and which may create potential conflicts of interest. The members of the\n795: Board were aware of and considered these interests in reaching the determination to approve the merger agreement and deem the merger agreement, the merger and the other transactions contemplated by the merger agreement to be advisable and in the\n796: best interests of the Company, and in recommending that the Company’s shareholders vote for the approval of the merger agreement. These interests include \n797: \n798:  • \n799: \n800:  each Company stock option and restricted stock unit, whether vested or unvested, will be canceled and converted into the right to receive cash payments based on the merger consideration upon the effective time of the\n801: merger in accordance with the terms of the merger agreement; \n802: \n803:  • \n804: \n805:  each of the Company’s executive officers (other than Robert H. Eder, our Chief Executive Officer) is party to a change in control agreement with the Company that provides severance in the case of a termination\n806: of employment by the Company without “cause” or by the executive for “good reason” in connection with or following a change in control, which will include the completion of the merger; and \n807: \n808:  • \n809: \n810:  the Company’s directors and executive officers are entitled to continued indemnification and insurance coverage under the merger agreement. \n811: \n812:  6 \n813: \n814:  Table of Contents \n815: \n816:  The merger agreement also provides that the Company’s executive officers immediately prior to the\n817: effective time of the merger (excluding our Chief Executive Officer) will remain in office following the merger until their successors are duly elected or appointed and qualified. Please see the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal\n818: No. 1—The Merger—Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger” beginning on page 44 for additional information about these financial interests. \n819: \n820:  Conditions to the Merger (page 52) \n821: \n822:  The obligations of G&W and the Company to effect the merger are subject to the satisfaction or waiver, at or prior to the closing of the\n823: merger, of a number of conditions, including: \n824: \n825:  • \n826: \n827:  holders of at least a majority of the outstanding shares of common stock and preferred stock, each voting separately as a class, have approved the merger agreement and the plan of merger; \n828: \n829:  • \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 830]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 250, \"offset\": 580, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/pw/filing.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824940085,"end":1789824940255}}}}
